      {
        "key": "achrony",
        "name": "Achrony",
        "works": 10,
        "definition": "You cannot confidently assign chronological ranks because the text gives no dates, no ageing, no causal signposts, and no other ordering cues. Stop here only if the order is genuinely unrecoverable, not merely withheld until a late reveal.",
        "description": "The corpus uses a narrower operational label than narratology: the chronology must remain reconstructable, while the telling follows no statable ordering pattern and is not merely awaiting a late reveal. The line wanders. The definition the models saw describes unrecoverable chronology, which the corpus excludes.",
        "example": "In the broader narratological sense, achrony describes a telling whose temporal relations cannot readily be established, as in Robbe-Grillet’s La Jalousie.",
        "exampleWorks": [
          "La Jalousie"
        ],
